\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Symbolic confirmation bias as unifying root cause.}
The oracle experiments causally identify the failure source: gold constraints yield $+14.22$\,pp on FOLIO and $+11.50$\,pp on ProofWriter ($p < 0.001$) while self-extraction captures only 24\% of this ceiling (\S\ref{sec:oracle}). Symbolic confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}) unifies the two channels through which trace correlation degrades SICA. The \emph{direct} channel limits voting diversity (Eff-$K < 2$ for many conditions); the \emph{indirect} channel operates through constraint quality, as correlated traces produce homogeneous constraints that cause MAX-SAT to degenerate to vote counting (Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}). Both stem from a single mechanism: the model generates an answer, then extracts FOL consistent with that answer. More than twenty remediation strategies fail because each addresses a symptom---diversity, filtering, alternative solvers---rather than this root cause.

\paragraph{Process-level implications.}
The gap between process-level similarity ($0.69$) and answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$) implies that the independence bottleneck is deeper than answer-level metrics suggest (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}). Interventions that successfully spread answers across candidates may still fail to diversify the underlying reasoning, explaining why temperature scaling and diverse prompting produce no SICA improvement despite affecting answer distributions.

\paragraph{Practitioner diagnostics.}
$C_0$ combined with SC accuracy predicts when symbolic augmentation is unlikely to help: $C_0 < 5\%$ implies degeneration; SC $\geq 60\%$ implies confirmation bias; SC $\leq 40\%$ implies error amplification. The $\kappa$-based Eff-$K$ metric quantifies SC's own scaling ceiling: at Eff-$K \approx 2$ (typical at $K = 12$), additional traces add negligible independent information.

\paragraph{Statistical evidence and scope.}
Zero of nine SICA conditions reaches $p < 0.05$, while three method-level conditions and two dataset conditions reach significance, all negative. Oracle controls remain highly significant ($p < 0.001$). Within the Qwen family, $\kappa$ increases from 0.66 (7B) to 0.74 (14B); whether 70B+ models or trained extractors shift the Pareto frontier is open. Published systems that report positive results \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm} each bypass same-model self-extraction, consistent with our diagnosis. Three paths forward trade simplicity for independence: trained extractors, external provers, and multi-model pipelines.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We evaluated SICA across six models (three families), three reasoning domains, and over 3,000 instances with four-seed replication. The root cause of self-extraction failure is symbolic confirmation bias: on incorrect problems, 83.3\% of self-extracted constraints support the wrong majority answer (bias ratio $4.97\times$), and 94\% of SICA errors overlap with SC (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Process-level analysis reveals a deeper bottleneck: chain-of-thought cosine similarity ($0.69$) far exceeds answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$), indicating reasoning homogeneity beyond what answer distributions suggest (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}).

This manifests as a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off. More than twenty remediation strategies all fail to escape the Pareto frontier: methods that break trace correlation (Debate: $\kappa = 0.15$) destroy accuracy ($-18.6$\,pp), while methods preserving accuracy cannot push below $\kappa \approx 0.40$---a ceiling universal across three model families. Oracle controls confirm the aggregation mechanism is sound ($+14.22$\,pp FOLIO, $+11.50$\,pp ProofWriter, $p < 0.001$); self-extraction captures only 24\% of this ceiling. For practitioners, $C_0$ combined with SC accuracy provides a zero-cost diagnostic predicting when symbolic augmentation is unlikely to help. Three paths forward trade simplicity for independence: trained extractors, external provers, and multi-model pipelines.
